*----------------------------------------------------------------------- 1 probe
* SN-26-spl-bridge-b — exercises all three SPITBOL monitor bridge sites:
*   b_vrs  (universal var-store)  → sysmv (VALUE)
*   bpf09  (function-call gate)   → sysmc (CALL)
*   retrn  (return path)          → sysmr (RETURN)
*
* Drive via:
*   MONITOR_READY_PIPE=...  MONITOR_GO_PIPE=...  MONITOR_NAMES_OUT=... \
*       /home/claude/x64/bin/sbl -bf probe.sno
*
* Expected wire (6 records on SPITBOL):
*   #0 VALUE  S    STRING(11)='hello world'      (b_vrs)
*   #1 CALL   SQR                                (bpf09)
*   #2 VALUE  SQR  INTEGER(49)                   (b_vrs inside body)
*   #3 RETURN SQR  STRING(6)='RETURN'            (retrn: &RTNTYPE)
*   #4 VALUE  N    INTEGER(49)                   (b_vrs)
*   #5 END
*
* Note SPITBOL diverges from CSNOBOL4 on this probe by ONE record:
* CSNOBOL4 fires monitor_emit_value at SJSRV1 for the pattern-substitute
* store `S 'world' = 'there'`, but SPITBOL's pattern-match store path
* doesn't traverse b_vrs.  This is a known asymmetry — captured as
* SN-26-spl-bridge-c (deferred).  For cross-oracle comparison purposes,
* either both probes drop the pattern-substitute line, or the controller
* allows the asymmetry.

        DEFINE('SQR(N)')                        :(SQR_END)
SQR     SQR = N * N                             :(RETURN)
SQR_END
        S = 'hello world'
        N = SQR(7)
END
*--------------------------------------------------------------------- 2 probe_b
* SN-26-csn-bridge-b — exercises all four CSNOBOL4 monitor bridge sites:
*   ASGNVV (plain X = Y)        → monitor_emit_value
*   SJSRV1 (S 'pat' = 'rep')    → monitor_emit_value
*   DEFF18 (function call)      → monitor_emit_call
*   DEFF20 (function return)    → monitor_emit_return
*
* Drive via:
*   MONITOR_READY_PIPE=...  MONITOR_GO_PIPE=...  MONITOR_NAMES_OUT=... \
*       /home/claude/csnobol4/snobol4 -bf probe_b.sno
*
* Expected wire (7 records):
*   #0 VALUE  S    STRING(11)='hello world'      (ASGNVV)
*   #1 VALUE  S    STRING(11)='hello there'      (SJSRV1)
*   #2 CALL   SQR                                (DEFF18)
*   #3 VALUE  SQR  INTEGER(49)                   (ASGNVV inside body)
*   #4 RETURN SQR  STRING(6)='RETURN'            (DEFF20: &RTNTYPE)
*   #5 VALUE  N    INTEGER(49)                   (ASGNVV)
*   #6 END

        DEFINE('SQR(N)')                        :(SQR_END)
SQR     SQR = N * N                             :(RETURN)
SQR_END
        S = 'hello world'
        S 'world' = 'there'
        N = SQR(7)
END
*--------------------------------------------------------------------- 3 probe_c
* SN-26-bridge-coverage-a — exercises the new NMD4 fire-point in CSNOBOL4
* monitor bridge:
*   NMD4 (pattern .-capture commit) → monitor_emit_value
*
* Drive via:
*   MONITOR_READY_PIPE=...  MONITOR_GO_PIPE=...  MONITOR_NAMES_OUT=... \
*       /home/claude/csnobol4/snobol4 -bf probe_c.sno
*
* Expected wire (3 records):
*   #0 VALUE  S         STRING(5)='AXBYC'      (ASGNVV — plain assignment)
*   #1 VALUE  captured  STRING(1)='A'          (NMD4   — pattern .-capture)
*   #2 END
*
* The middle record is the new one — proves NMD4 fires on a successful
* match's ANY('AB') . captured commit point.

        S = 'AXBYC'
        S ANY('AB') . captured
END
*----------------------------------------------------------------- 4 probe_label
* SN-26-bridge-coverage-f — exercise the new MWK_LABEL fire-point.
*
* Three top-level statements + END.  Each runtime emits one LABEL
* record per stmt entry, with stno = 1, 2, 3 in order on the wire.
* CSNOBOL4 fires LABEL only on user statements (3 LABELs).
* SPITBOL counts END as a stmt (4 LABELs: 1, 2, 3, 4).
* scrip --run matches CSN; --run / --run match SPL.
*
* The drivers (test_smoke_sn26_label_flow*.sh) verify each runtime emits
* its expected LABEL stream interleaved with VALUE records on assignments.

        X = 1
        Y = 2
        OUTPUT = X + Y
END
